### Step 5: Launch EC2 instances and install Docker

EC2 hosted Docker container with HTML & pushing image to ECR | by Bjorn Nielsen | Medium

[medium.com](<https://medium.com/%40bnalaska96/docker-with-html-image-pushed-to-ecr-4a0244781061?utm_source=chatgpt.com>)

Now we launch EC2 instances in private application subnets.

We install Docker Engine on the instances.

We also configure IAM instance roles so that EC2 can pull Docker images from ECR.

The deployment process is:

1. EC2 authenticates with Amazon ECR using its IAM role.
2. EC2 pulls the required Docker image.
3. Docker creates the container.
4. The application starts inside the container.
5. We expose the application port internally.
6. We configure health checks.

Example:

bash

```bash
docker pull ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/my-backend:latest
```

Run the container:

bash

```bash
docker run -d \
--name my-backend \
-p 8080:8080 \
ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.REGION.amazonaws.com/my-backend:latest
```

For production, we use immutable image tags, such as a Git commit ID or build number, instead of relying on `latest`.

### Step 6: Configure the Application Load Balancer

Now we create an AWS Application Load Balancer.

The ALB receives incoming user requests and forwards them to healthy application targets.

For example:

text

```text
https://myapplication.com
        |
        v
Application Load Balancer
        |
        |---- Frontend target group
        |       |
        |       v
        |    Frontend container
        |
        |---- Backend target group
                |
                v
             .NET 8 API
```

We can configure host-based or path-based routing.

For example:

- `/` routes to the frontend.
- `/api/*` routes to the backend API.

We configure HTTPS using an ACM certificate.

We also configure health check endpoints, such as `/health`.

Important: When using EC2 instances as ALB targets, we can register the EC2 instance and the port on which the container is exposed.

